IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Companies Act 1967 (Singapore): the ONE place where the Act's periods are counted.
-- Imports prelude and daydate only. Every part calls these functions; no part writes its own date arithmetic.
--
-- WHERE THE ACT COUNTS. The Act has one counting rule of its own in Part 1: s 4(1) "business day"
-- (any day other than a Saturday, Sunday or public holiday), implemented below. It says nothing else about how a
-- period is counted: no rule for "within N days after", for "not less than N days before", for a month.
--
-- WHERE THE ACT IS SILENT. The Interpretation Act 1965 is not among the sources (and no number is read from memory),
-- so the counting convention below is an ASSUMPTION, NOT RULED BY A LAWYER, and each choice has a fork line in NOTES.md
-- (fork register (keel), P1 to P9). The conventions taken:
--   P1  the day of the event is not counted: day 1 of "N days after X" is the day after X, so the last day is X plus N.
--   P2  "within N days after X" is a window that opens the day after X: an act on X itself is not "within" the window
--       (period: the date is within a period after), while an act on any day up to the last day is "not later than"
--       the last day (period: the date is not later than the end of a period after).
--   P3  a period of weeks is 7 days a week.
--   P4  a period of months or years ends on the corresponding date of the last month; where that month has no such
--       date it ends on the month's last day (31 August plus 6 months is 28 February). daydate's `add months` clamps this way.
--   P5  "not less than N days before X": the last day on which the act may be done is X minus N (X not counted, the day of the act counted).
--   P6  "within N months before X" runs from the corresponding date N months earlier up to the day before X.
--   P7  "N clear days" excludes both the day of the event and the day of the act (offered as the alternative to P1 and P5).
--   P8  business days are counted from the day after the event, skipping weekends and the supplied public holidays.
--   P9  a count that is not a whole non-negative number is not checked here: the caller supplies N from the Act's own words.
-- The first four conventions are the ones a statute counts by when it says nothing; P7 is offered, never defaulted.
--
-- A date is written Date day month year. A period count is a NUMBER; the unit is one of the four constructors.

DECLARE `Period unit` IS ONE OF
    Days
    Weeks
    Months
    Years

§ `The last day of a period after an event`

-- "within N days (weeks, months, years) after X": the last day on which the act may be done.
-- P1, P3, P4. `add months` and `add years` clamp to the last day of a short month.
GIVEN `the day of the event` IS A DATE
      `the number of units` IS A NUMBER
      `the unit` IS A `Period unit`
GIVETH A DATE
`period: the last day of a period after` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the unit`
    WHEN Days THEN `the day of the event` PLUS `the number of units`
    WHEN Weeks THEN `the day of the event` PLUS (7 TIMES `the number of units`)
    WHEN Months THEN `add months` `the day of the event` `the number of units`
    WHEN Years THEN `add years` `the day of the event` `the number of units`

-- "within N units after X" as a window that opens the day after X (P2): TRUE from the day after X to the last day.
GIVEN `the day of the event` IS A DATE
      `the day the act is done` IS A DATE
      `the number of units` IS A NUMBER
      `the unit` IS A `Period unit`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`period: the date is within a period after` MEANS
        `the day the act is done` GREATER THAN `the day of the event`
    AND `the day the act is done` AT MOST
        `period: the last day of a period after` `the day of the event` `the number of units` `the unit`

-- "not later than N units after X" (P2): TRUE on every day up to and including the last day; an early act is in time.
GIVEN `the day of the event` IS A DATE
      `the day the act is done` IS A DATE
      `the number of units` IS A NUMBER
      `the unit` IS A `Period unit`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`period: the date is not later than the end of a period after` MEANS
    `the day the act is done` AT MOST
        `period: the last day of a period after` `the day of the event` `the number of units` `the unit`

§ `A period before an event`

-- "not less than N units before X" (P5, P4): the last day on which the act may be done.
GIVEN `the day of the event` IS A DATE
      `the number of units` IS A NUMBER
      `the unit` IS A `Period unit`
GIVETH A DATE
`period: the latest day for an act a period before` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the unit`
    WHEN Days THEN `the day of the event` MINUS `the number of units`
    WHEN Weeks THEN `the day of the event` MINUS (7 TIMES `the number of units`)
    WHEN Months THEN `add months` `the day of the event` (0 MINUS `the number of units`)
    WHEN Years THEN `add years` `the day of the event` (0 MINUS `the number of units`)

-- "not less than N units before X": TRUE when the act is done on or before the latest day.
GIVEN `the day the act is done` IS A DATE
      `the day of the event` IS A DATE
      `the number of units` IS A NUMBER
      `the unit` IS A `Period unit`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`period: the date is at least a period before` MEANS
    `the day the act is done` AT MOST
        `period: the latest day for an act a period before` `the day of the event` `the number of units` `the unit`

-- "within N units before X" (P6): from the corresponding date N units earlier up to the day before X.
GIVEN `the day the act is done` IS A DATE
      `the day of the event` IS A DATE
      `the number of units` IS A NUMBER
      `the unit` IS A `Period unit`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`period: the date is within a period before` MEANS
        `the day the act is done` LESS THAN `the day of the event`
    AND `the day the act is done` AT LEAST
        `period: the latest day for an act a period before` `the day of the event` `the number of units` `the unit`

§ `Clear days (P7): the alternative, offered and never defaulted`

-- "N clear days" before X: neither the day of the act nor X is counted, so the latest day is X minus N minus 1.
GIVEN `the day of the event` IS A DATE
      `the number of clear days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`period: the latest day for an act at least a number of clear days before` MEANS
    (`the day of the event` MINUS `the number of clear days`) MINUS 1

-- "after N clear days" from X: neither X nor the day of the act is counted, so the earliest day is X plus N plus 1.
GIVEN `the day of the event` IS A DATE
      `the number of clear days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`period: the earliest day for an act after a number of clear days from` MEANS
    (`the day of the event` PLUS `the number of clear days`) PLUS 1

§ `Calendar months`

-- "the last day of the month": the last day of the calendar month the date falls in.
GIVEN `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`period: the last day of the calendar month containing` MEANS
    Date (`Days in month` (DATE_MONTH `the date`) (DATE_YEAR `the date`)) (DATE_MONTH `the date`) (DATE_YEAR `the date`)

§ `Business days: the Act's own counting rule, s 4(1) "business day"`

-- The public holidays are supplied: the Act does not list them (they are fixed under another Act, not among the sources).
-- src:1239 | “business day” means any day other than a Saturday, Sunday or
-- src:1240 | public holiday;
@ref s 4(1) "business day" (src:1239-1240)
GIVEN `the date` IS A DATE
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`period: the date is a business day` MEANS
    (NOT (`is weekend` `the date`)) AND (NOT (elem `the date` `the public holidays`))

-- s 4(1) "business day", as the Act words it: a "means" definition, so a day that is a Saturday, Sunday or public holiday is not one.
@ref s 4(1) "business day" (src:1239-1240)
GIVEN `the date` IS A DATE
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(1) "business day" the date is a business day` MEANS
    IF `period: the date is a business day` `the date` `the public holidays`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE  -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "means any day other than a Saturday, Sunday or public holiday"

-- P8: walk forward one calendar day at a time, counting only business days, until the count is reached.
GIVEN `the day counted from` IS A DATE
      `the business days still to count` IS A NUMBER
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`period: the day reached counting business days` MEANS
    IF `the business days still to count` AT MOST 0
    THEN `the day counted from`
    ELSE IF `period: the date is a business day` (`the day counted from` PLUS 1) `the public holidays`
         THEN `period: the day reached counting business days` (`the day counted from` PLUS 1) (`the business days still to count` MINUS 1) `the public holidays`
         ELSE `period: the day reached counting business days` (`the day counted from` PLUS 1) `the business days still to count` `the public holidays`

-- "within N business days after X": the last day is the Nth business day after X (P8).
GIVEN `the day of the event` IS A DATE
      `the number of business days` IS A NUMBER
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`period: the last day of a number of business days after` MEANS
    `period: the day reached counting business days` `the day of the event` `the number of business days` `the public holidays`

-- The window form (P2): after the event, up to and including the Nth business day.
GIVEN `the day of the event` IS A DATE
      `the day the act is done` IS A DATE
      `the number of business days` IS A NUMBER
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`period: the date is within a number of business days after` MEANS
        `the day the act is done` GREATER THAN `the day of the event`
    AND `the day the act is done` AT MOST
        `period: the last day of a number of business days after` `the day of the event` `the number of business days` `the public holidays`
